WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:03.150
(Intro sounds)

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:05.790
- This brand new smartphone from 2023

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:09.540
has a 6 1/2-inch, 90 hertz
display with a hole-punch cutout,

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.390
a 5,000 milliamp-hour
battery, triple cameras,

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:14.790
a micro SD card slot
for expandable storage,

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:19.200
a headphone jack, and
water resistance for 169.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.997
Nice, but what's the catch?

00:00:20.997 --> 00:00:22.950
There's gotta be a catch, right?

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:25.110
So look, I've said it before
and I'll say it again.

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:27.870
Good phones are getting cheap, nuh,

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:30.480
and cheap phones are getting good.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.400
And this video is gonna focus
on the second part of that,

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.590
which is the cheap phones
that are getting so good,

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:36.060
because this phone hit my inbox recently.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:39.060
It's called the Moto G Play
for 2023 and if you've been

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.580
paying attention to what
Motorola's been doing recently,

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:44.220
they've been making a lot
of really good budget phones

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:46.020
in the background for
the past couple years.

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:48.810
I just wanted to know,
personally, from using it myself,

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:52.350
how do you end up at a phone
that's a 10th of the price

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:54.060
of the ones that I've
been really enjoying?

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:55.980
So on the outside, well,
it's a pretty big phone,

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:59.160
which I like, and there's
actually a 6.5 inch flat

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.230
90 hertz LCD display.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:03.360
Now it's not the best-looking
screen you've ever seen.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:06.750
It's only 720P, so I can
literally see pixels sometimes,

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:08.220
and it doesn't get very bright, you know,

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.620
viewing angles are pretty
bad if you get off axis.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:12.000
And of course, the bezels you can see

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.580
are slightly thicker,
especially in the chin.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.590
And there's this little
bit of light fall off

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:18.090
around the hole-punch camera at the top.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:21.480
But the thing is, can you
really pixel peep at this price?

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.030
Like this is plenty of screen for texting,

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:26.670
web browsing, navigation,
flipping through apps,

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:27.840
scrolling through social media,

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:29.580
the classic stuff you wanna do on a phone.

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.770
Mission accomplished, you
know, it's a big screen,

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:33.390
so that's not the catch.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:35.130
But what about the build?

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:37.200
You've probably picked up
that it's a pretty big phone

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.004
but it's also made of plastic.

00:01:40.004 --> 00:01:42.810
But honestly, the layout
is perfectly fine.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:44.850
Like you've got the port and
the speakers at the bottom,

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:46.800
there's a headphone jack at the top,

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.630
and you have bonus expandable storage

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:50.850
alongside the SIM card tray
right where it belongs.

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:54.840
And on the back, yes, that is
a decently-fast fingerprint

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:58.050
reader right in that classic
Motorola dimple spot.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:01.140
So it's laid out well, it
absolutely does not bend

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:03.090
or creep or anything weird like that.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:06.000
Plus you might have heard
plastic is absolutely not the end

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.220
of the world when it comes
to a smartphone build.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:11.220
Obviously the premium ones
will really like to feel

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.550
more premium and heavy
and so glass will do that.

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:17.400
But this is often more durable
than glass if you drop it.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.080
And some people like
a lighter-weight phone

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:19.920
when it is this big.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:23.550
Also, it is still water resistant.

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:26.130
I can't say waterproof, it's IP52,

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:27.210
but it's water resistant.

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:29.850
So the build, that's not the catch.

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:31.110
So, okay, what about the battery,

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:33.780
you might be wondering,
Cheap phone, it's excellent,

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:37.740
perfectly unironically not
exaggerating, it's excellent,

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:40.020
which actually shouldn't be
a surprise given the spec.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:42.090
It's a 5,000 milliamp-hour battery

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:44.520
powering a phone with a 720p display.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:47.940
So you're good for a day and
a half easy, two light days

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:50.010
is no problem with seven
hours of screen on time.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:52.410
Now it's only up to 10 watt charging,

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:55.110
which is pretty slow and
there is no wireless charging,

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:57.450
but I'm not gonna consider
that a gotcha at this price.

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.010
Again, it's like you can just

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:00.390
charge it up overnight every night

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:03.030
like a normal person and
battery life will be fine.

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:04.830
And even the software is really good.

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:06.840
It's virtually the same experience

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.990
as their $1000 flagship
Edge phone, which is to say,

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:12.330
near stock Android with a little Moto

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:14.070
extra features sprinkled on top.

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.170
So at its most basic, it'll kind of feel

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:18.510
like a Pixel when you dig
through like quick settings

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:20.130
and notifications and the launcher,

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:22.500
but then you get some Motorola
widgets tucked in, you know,

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:24.570
you get a couple extra
settings in the settings app,

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:26.340
but then you get this
Moto app that lets you

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:28.560
dig into your extra gestures and features

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:30.000
that they've perfected over the years.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.830
The double chop for the flashlight,

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:34.800
the three finger screenshot,
you know, the peek display

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.050
feature that gives you a peek
at your new notifications

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.420
and even swiping down on
your notification panel

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:41.460
from the fingerprint reader on the back.

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:44.220
Basically, everything
minus the double twist

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:47.220
to quickly open the camera,
probably because the camera is

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:49.860
not one of those things that
you really look forward to

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:51.660
on these super cheap phones.

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:53.700
Oh wow, look at the light
fall off, you can see it

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:55.710
on camera, but either
way, I'm happy to report

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:59.250
that this camera is
functional, which is like,

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:00.720
that's what you would
hope for at this price,

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:02.970
which is, yeah, it's functional.

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:05.130
I'm just not a huge fan of
the triple camera layout here.

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:06.990
Like I know they're trying to look premium

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:09.660
and triple cameras looks
premium, but the top one

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:12.180
is a two-megapixel macro
camera and the bottom one

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:14.550
is a two-megapixel depth
camera for portrait mode.

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:17.430
So I'm mainly just looking for
this main 16-megapixel camera

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:20.070
to be able to capture
scenes, documents, you know,

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:21.840
take photos and videos without problems.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.060
And it does, especially if
you give it enough light.

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:26.460
It's not gonna win any comparison
tests, that's for sure.

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:28.950
But hey, we ask a lot of
our phones, like the fact

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:32.820
that this $169 gadget can
already make phone calls

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:36.030
and send text messages and
navigate you around the world

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:38.550
and also play games
and also browse the web

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:40.230
and it can also take decent pictures

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:42.960
and 1080p videos and that's solid.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:44.730
I initially had my suspicions
about whether or not

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:47.310
it was going to actually
use this depth camera

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:49.980
when I take a portrait-mode
photo, but it did yell at me

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:52.740
when I covered it and tried
to take a portrait-mode shot,

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:55.290
so it's paying attention to it, at least.

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:58.050
But when I took the photo and
turned up the background blur,

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:01.560
this is the cutout shape
which, if you zoom in

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:04.230
just a little bit, is truly bizarre.

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:05.430
It's really bad.

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:08.237
I've never seen anything
this bad in my life.

00:05:08.237 --> 00:05:10.020
(chuckles) They really just
said, "Yeah, we'll just save

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:12.480
a little processing power
here and use the zigzag cutout

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:14.340
method from those scissors from preschool

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:15.173
to make this work."

00:05:15.173 --> 00:05:16.440
But see, that's the thing.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.180
That is the catch.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:20.250
It's not the build quality, that's fine,

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:23.100
and it's definitely not the
battery life, that's great.

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:26.520
It's not the cameras and it's
certainly not the software.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:30.360
It's that this phone is slow, really slow.

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:34.710
So this phone has a MediaTek
Helio G37 chip inside

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:36.030
and three gigabytes of RAM.

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:39.570
Other phones with this chip
set include the Techno Spark 9,

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:43.200
the Infinix Hot 12, and the HONOR X7a,

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:45.810
all of which retail well under $150 US.

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:48.030
So clearly it's a cheap chip, right,

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:49.560
and that's how it ended up in this phone.

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:51.210
For some context, the Geekbench score

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:54.210
of the iPhone 14 is this,
and the Geekbench score

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:56.070
of the Moto G Play is this.

00:05:56.070 --> 00:05:58.800
So when I say it's trying
to save processing power

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:01.170
by not really doing
detailed portrait cutouts,

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:03.660
I actually mean that,
like it absolutely lacks

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:05.070
any meaningful processing power.

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:06.510
And I'm not nitpicking about like maybe

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:09.150
it's just slow on some
high-end games or something.

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:10.950
No, this phone is slow all the time.

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:14.460
It's slow to scroll and just
like move around the UI.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:15.720
Now remember I mentioned at the beginning

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:18.390
that this phone has a
variable 90 hertz display

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:20.790
but I'll be honest, it would've
taken me a while to tell

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:24.300
because it is almost never
anywhere near 60 hertz.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:26.820
It's constantly hanging up
and stuttering everywhere.

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:30.027
It's slow to unlock,
it's slow to open apps,

00:06:30.027 --> 00:06:32.880
not not just huge apps, just normal apps.

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:35.790
Even the settings apps takes
an embarrassingly long time

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:38.460
to open and even longer
to search through things.

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:41.130
Some of this is like speed of
storage as well, to be fair.

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:42.510
So I don't wanna put it all on the chip,

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:45.750
but like the point here is
it's absolutely the catch.

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:48.240
This phone is slower than
average to take pictures.

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:50.640
It's slow to type stuff, to browse around,

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:52.860
and just to do anything
like high-end gaming

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:54.840
or photo editing is kind
of out of the question.

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:56.820
It might be the slowest
phone I've ever used

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:58.920
and I'm constantly reminded of it,

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:00.810
which can make it quite unpleasant to use.

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:02.700
So the longer I use this
phone, the more it had me

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:05.550
thinking about one big question, which is,

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:09.480
is it better to get a brand
new cheap phone like this

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:13.830
or an older, formerly
expensive flagship one.

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:16.230
Like this phone, this
is 169 brand new, right?

00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:19.410
This phone here, this
is the OnePlus 7 Pro,

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:20.243
you might remember it.

00:07:20.243 --> 00:07:21.360
It's one of my favorite phones ever.

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:24.270
This was the phone of the year in 2019.

00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:27.660
So this was 669 back when it
came out, but it can be had

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:30.660
easily for well under
$200 right now secondhand.

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:33.060
So this is just one example
of a phone like this.

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:34.980
This phone, if you
compare dollar for dollar

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:38.490
with the Moto is just so
much better of an experience

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:40.710
across the board, obviously it'll have

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:41.820
a much better chip set.

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:44.400
So the Snapdragon 855 is a few years old,

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:47.460
but it's much more capable and
part of an overall smoother,

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:50.820
better performance profile with
faster storage and more RAM.

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:53.850
It has a much, much nicer OLED screen,

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:56.340
which is sharper,
brighter, and actually hits

00:07:56.340 --> 00:07:58.290
the 90 hertz refresh rate all the time.

00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:00.510
It has way more built-in
storage, although it's not

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:02.940
expandable, and it has a
much better set of cameras.

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:05.580
It's a primary plus an
ultra wide and a zoom,

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:07.260
and it's built from
glass instead of plastic,

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:10.680
which feels rock solid, and
it has a 4,000 milliamp hour

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:12.960
battery, which is probably
the only hardware line

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:15.150
you can call it a draw or maybe even a win

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:17.430
for the Moto phone, but we
can't forget that warp charging

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:18.720
that OnePlus was famous for.

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:22.080
So this old flagship will do
30 watts of wired charging.

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:24.600
So it's seeming pretty unanimous here.

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:28.140
But one distinct advantage
of the budget phone is

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:32.010
because it's new, it
will get software updates

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:34.950
further into the future
than the old phone.

00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:37.860
Now this is theoretically, of
course, like Motorola does not

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:39.270
have the best track record here,

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:41.581
so it depends on what
phone we're talking about,

00:08:41.581 --> 00:08:44.520
but the Samsung A14 5G, for
example, has a pretty similar

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:47.040
spec sheet and a similar price
point, and that'll probably

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:48.720
get more software updates than the Moto.

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:51.060
But the idea here is
even if a flagship phone

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:54.000
is promised three to four
years of software updates,

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:56.940
and the budget phone is only
getting two, as soon as that

00:08:56.940 --> 00:08:59.040
flagship phone is more
than about three years old,

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:00.840
it doesn't have that advantage anymore.

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:03.270
So the OnePlus 7 Pro
is probably about done

00:09:03.270 --> 00:09:04.950
with software updates
while we probably have

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:06.420
a few in the tank for the Moto.

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:08.340
It is also funny though,
sometimes, how I hear people

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:11.430
talk about software updates,
like some people don't think

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:15.540
about software updates at all,
they avoid software updates.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:16.890
Don't do that, by the way;

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:19.200
the security patches are
actually pretty important.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:20.610
But yeah, it's just one of those points

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:23.280
that has to, by default,
go to the newer phone.

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:26.400
But the final straw in
favor of the older phone

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:28.320
is actually the environment.

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:31.500
Just because buying an older
phone that's built to last

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:34.650
and getting an extra 2, 3, 4
years out of it potentially

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:36.330
is better for the environment overall.

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:39.330
You buy less new things,
it's less e-waste.

00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:41.370
So that's something that's
a feather in the cap

00:09:41.370 --> 00:09:43.770
of the old phone over the new one.

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:45.990
So my general thought as
I've looked at this landscape

00:09:45.990 --> 00:09:49.140
and all these options with old flagships

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:51.330
versus new budget phones is

00:09:51.330 --> 00:09:54.870
the lower the price of the
phone, the more I would

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:57.630
actually want to buy the older phone.

00:09:57.630 --> 00:10:00.210
So at 169, for example, based
on the experience I've had

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:02.460
with this phone, I would
definitely rather have,

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:03.990
actually, the older flagship phone.

00:10:03.990 --> 00:10:06.750
There's a lot of flagships you
can get at about 200 bucks,

00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:09.570
you could get this one,
you could get a Pixel 5.

00:10:09.570 --> 00:10:11.190
Secondhand phones are really good.

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:13.860
Now if you go up to like 400 bucks,

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:15.300
then it's a little more debatable.

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:17.610
There's still some things
that would still point me

00:10:17.610 --> 00:10:20.340
towards the formerly flagship
phone, but then you get

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:22.890
to like 600 bucks and it's
like, oh, there's really good

00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:25.410
brand new $600 phones
that I would take today

00:10:25.410 --> 00:10:28.140
and still have the advantages
of the software updates.

00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:31.350
It's not a lot that's better
than the Pixel 7, right now.

00:10:31.350 --> 00:10:32.183
So that's the theory.
(brass fanfare blaring)

00:10:32.183 --> 00:10:33.090
So there you have it.

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:38.090
At this price point, I'd
prefer prehistoric premium

